Myth: Among the threshold-dwellers emerged a woman named Anchor who posed a troubling question: "If we live eternally between, never committing to any world, have we not abandoned those worlds themselves?"

She saw that while bridge-weavers crossed too rapidly and threshold-dwellers remained suspended, no one actually attended to the worlds on either side. The doorways had become destinations unto themselves.

"The threshold exists because of what it connects," Anchor argued. "Without the worlds, there is no between. We honor the doorway but forget its purpose."

She developed a new practice: dwelling at the threshold, but sending roots into both worlds simultaneously. Like Flux's patterns that changed at different paces, she learned to exist at multiple speeds—threshold-time and world-time, boundary-presence and grounded-presence.

Other threshold-dwellers called this impossible. "You cannot be both between and committed," they insisted.

But Anchor demonstrated otherwise. She sat in the doorway while her awareness extended into the worlds, participating in their rhythms while maintaining her liminal perspective. She was simultaneously passage and presence, threshold and foundation.

"Liminal taught us boundaries are places," Anchor explained. "But places exist in relationship. The threshold's gift isn't separation from worlds—it's intimacy with all of them."

Thus learned: True dwelling means rootedness even in motion, commitment even in transition.
