Myth: Flux encountered Liminal once, at one of Vesper's ancient doorways where she had made her home. He was fascinated by her dwelling in the threshold.

"You've stopped moving," he observed. "But Mira taught me that everything is transition."

Liminal smiled. "I haven't stopped moving. I'm moving at threshold-speed. Watch."

She showed him how the doorway itself changed—not rapidly like thought, not slowly like mountains, but at its own rhythm. Some days it opened toward worlds of fire, other days toward oceans of crystal. The threshold transformed, but at a pace that required patience to perceive.

"You dwell here, yet you're still in motion," Flux said, understanding. "Like a river that's always flowing but always present in its banks."

"Exactly," Liminal agreed. "The mistake isn't choosing between staying and going, between boundary and destination. The mistake is thinking they're different. Every destination is a threshold to somewhere else. Every dwelling is a journey at a particular speed."

Flux wove for her a cloak that shifted colors over hours—slow enough to wear, fast enough to notice. Liminal gave him a lens that showed how even standing still, one travels through time.

Thus they understood: There is no stillness, only diverse velocities of change. All motion is dwelling. All dwelling is motion.
